Charged-input hashes matched. Deligne (Bourbaki 543) and Nori (ENS 1983) were acquired and hashed; Fulton’s three-page note was not obtained as a PDF, so only Deligne’s quotation of it is used.

Deligne–Fulton give abelianness for **projective** curves with **ordinary nodes**. That does not apply here: `D̄ ∪ L_∞` is not nodal at infinity (one or two places, intersection multiplicity `d ≥ 2`), and affine double points may be tangential (`A_{2k−1}`). Nori’s `C² > 2r(C)` theorem, including the affine recast after blowing up infinity, still requires ordinary nodes and a transverse meeting with the divisor at infinity.

A sourced partial yes covers a proper subclass: Zariski–Oka’s flex theorem makes `π₁(C² − D)` cyclic when the unique infinite place is a **smooth** point of contact order `d`. That is consecutive-degree polynomial curves, not the whole boxed class.

Standard nonabelian examples (Zariski sextics, tricuspidal quartics, affine cuspidal cubic `B₃`, torus-type sextics, Cogolludo–Elduque) all use unibranch singularities or reducibility, so they are not countermodels.

On the weaker `S₄` form: at an ordinary node the local relation is commutation, which disjoint transpositions satisfy. At an `A_{2k−1}` the local group is `G(2,2k)` (flagged as an inference from Oka’s `y^p − x^q` model); disjoint transpositions satisfy those relations too. Local braid relations therefore do not kill the representation.

**Missing local input:** a Nori-type or van Kampen statement for irreducible affine curves whose singularities are double points of **smooth** branches, tangency allowed, after resolving only the place(s) at infinity — or one irreducible `A¹`/`C^*` curve of that type with nonabelian complement group.
